// In-depth explanations, weeks 5-8. Format: [question text prefix, deep explanation, [note for each option in written order]]
var DEEP = typeof DEEP !== "undefined" ? DEEP : [];
DEEP.push(
  // ---------- WEEK 5 ----------
  ["In a medical diagnosis task",
   "Worked example: 1,000 patients, 10 sick (1% prevalence). A useless model that says 'healthy' to everyone gets TN = 990, FN = 10, so accuracy = 990/1000 = 99%, yet sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) = 0/10 = 0%. Accuracy mixes the two error types and is dominated by the majority class. Sensitivity (how many sick are caught) and specificity (how many healthy are correctly cleared) each look at one class, so neither can be inflated by imbalance. Precision, recall and the ROC curve are also appropriate. MSE is for regression, and training loss says nothing about deployment.",
   ["Misleading under imbalance (the 99% trap above).",
    "Correct: per-class rates that are robust to imbalance.",
    "MSE is a regression metric.",
    "Training loss measures fit, not test performance."]],
  ["Which formula is correct",
   "Confusion matrix for one positive class: TP (sick, predicted sick), FN (sick, predicted healthy), FP (healthy, predicted sick), TN (healthy, predicted healthy). Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of everything flagged positive, the fraction truly positive. Recall = sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN): of all real positives, the fraction found. Specificity = TN/(TN+FP): of all real negatives, the fraction correctly rejected. Accuracy = (TP+TN)/(TP+TN+FP+FN). Memory aid: Precision looks along the Predicted column; Recall and sensitivity look along the Real positive row.",
   ["Correct.",
    "TN/(TN+FP) is specificity, not recall.",
    "TP/(TP+FN) is sensitivity/recall, not specificity.",
    "TP/(TP+FP) is precision; accuracy needs TN too."]],
  ["Which two metrics are the same thing",
   "Recall, sensitivity, true positive rate (TPR) and hit rate are all TP/(TP+FN): how many actual positives the model finds. Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) is the true negative rate, and 1 − specificity is the false positive rate used on the ROC x-axis. Precision = TP/(TP+FP) is also called positive predictive value (PPV). The lecture's question 'are there overlaps between these metrics?' expects recall = sensitivity.",
   ["Correct: both are TP/(TP+FN).",
    "Precision uses FP in the denominator; specificity uses the negatives.",
    "Recall is about positives; specificity is about negatives.",
    "Accuracy uses all four cells."]],
  ["A model gives TP = 40",
   "Step 1, precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 40/(40+20) = 40/60 = 0.667. Step 2, recall = TP/(TP+FN) = 40/(40+10) = 40/50 = 0.80. Bonus: specificity = TN/(TN+FP) = 930/950 = 0.979, and accuracy = (40+930)/1000 = 0.97. F1 = 2PR/(P+R) = 2(0.667)(0.8)/(1.467) ≈ 0.727. The 97% accuracy hides that one in three positive predictions is a false alarm.",
   ["Correct: P = 0.67, R = 0.80.",
    "Swapped.",
    "0.97 is accuracy, not precision.",
    "Uses wrong denominators."]],
  ["A false positive is also called",
   "False positive: predicting the condition when it is absent, a false alarm. In statistics it's a Type I error (rejecting a true null hypothesis); the lecture describes it as 'overdoing a task'. False negative: missing a real case (miss detection, Type II error, 'not very good at a task'). Example of asymmetric costs: in a pregnancy test, an FP causes stress and an FN delays care; in elderly fall detection, an FN can be fatal.",
   ["Correct: false alarm / Type I.",
    "That's the false negative.",
    "A true negative is a correct rejection.",
    "Sensitivity is a rate, not an error type."]],
  ["For a disease screening test",
   "Screening tests are cheap and applied to many people. Missing a case (FN) means the person is never followed up, so you choose a threshold with high sensitivity and accept more false alarms that later tests will filter. Diagnostic tests (e.g. a $2,000 PET scan for Alzheimer's after memory screening) must avoid false alarms, so they need high specificity. The operating point on the ROC curve is chosen by these costs, not by accuracy.",
   ["Correct: minimise misses.",
    "That's the goal for diagnosis, not screening.",
    "Accuracy ignores the asymmetric costs.",
    "Impossible in practice; there's always a trade-off."]],
  ["How is a ROC curve built",
   "Step by step: (1) the model outputs a continuous score (probability) for each test sample. (2) Choose a threshold t: score ≥ t is positive. (3) Compute TPR = sensitivity and FPR = 1 − specificity for that t. (4) Sweep t from high to low; each t gives a point, from (0,0) at a very strict threshold to (1,1) when everything is positive. The diagonal is random guessing; the top-left corner (0,1) is perfect. AUC is the probability that a random positive scores higher than a random negative.",
   ["Correct: sweep the threshold, plot TPR vs FPR.",
    "That's a training curve.",
    "That's not a ROC curve.",
    "That's a calibration (reliability) diagram."]],
  ["What is the effect of low disease prevalence",
   "TPR = TP/(TP+FN) uses only positives; FPR = FP/(FP+TN) uses only negatives. Changing how many positives exist relative to negatives doesn't change either rate, so the ROC curve is unchanged. Precision does change. Example: sensitivity = specificity = 98%. At 50% prevalence, PPV = 98%. At 1% prevalence in 10,000 people: TP = 98, FP = 0.02 × 9,900 = 198, so PPV = 98/296 ≈ 33%. That's why the lecture says low prevalence needs other metrics.",
   ["Correct: ROC is prevalence-independent.",
    "Not true.",
    "Not true.",
    "It can still be computed."]],
  ["What is a calibration curve",
   "Procedure: (1) run the model on a test set, and for each prediction store its confidence (max softmax probability) and whether it was correct. (2) Group predictions into confidence bins (0-10%, …, 90-100%). (3) For each bin compute the actual accuracy. (4) Plot accuracy against average confidence. A perfectly calibrated model lies on the diagonal: among predictions made with ~55% confidence, about 55% are correct. Below the diagonal is over-confident; above is under-confident. Expected Calibration Error (ECE) averages the gaps.",
   ["Correct: confidence vs accuracy per bin.",
    "That's per-class ROC.",
    "That's a learning-rate finder.",
    "That's a label histogram."]],
  ["Deep neural classifiers are often described",
   "Modern deep networks trained with cross-entropy keep increasing their logits to push the training loss towards 0, so their softmax confidences become extreme (often 99%+) even when the accuracy at that confidence is lower. On a calibration plot they sit below the diagonal. Fixes include temperature scaling (divide logits by T > 1, tuned on validation data), label smoothing, and ensembles.",
   ["Correct: over-confident.",
    "The usual problem is the opposite.",
    "Not the case.",
    "Softmax outputs are probabilities."]],
  ["What is the difference between feature shift",
   "Feature (covariate) shift: p(x) changes but the classes are the same. Images look different because of a new camera, weather, lighting or demographics, which is a form of data drift. Class shift (novel classes, open set): a new category appears that the model has never seen, e.g. a fox shown to a cat/dog classifier. Both are safety concerns because the model will still output a prediction, often with high confidence, rather than saying 'I don't know'.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Swapped.",
    "Unrelated to the learning rate.",
    "They occur at deployment."]],
  ["Why do deep learning classifiers often assign",
   "A linear last layer gives each class a score proportional to the input's signed distance from that class's boundary. A novel input (a fox) can lie far from the boundaries in feature space, which makes some known class score very large. Softmax then normalises over known classes only, so all probability mass goes to one of them (e.g. 'dog, 97%'). There's no 'none of the above' output. Remedies: out-of-distribution detection, density models p(x), ensembles, and reject options.",
   ["Correct: softmax has to choose among known classes, and far-away points get extreme scores.",
    "They don't detect novelty by themselves.",
    "Dropout is off at test time and isn't the cause.",
    "Classifiers use cross-entropy."]],
  ["At a high level, how is an adversarial",
   "Training computes ∂L/∂weights to change the model. An attack computes ∂L/∂x, the gradient with respect to the input image, with the weights fixed. FGSM, for example: x_adv = x + ε·sign(∇ₓL(x, y_true)). This moves every pixel slightly in the direction that most increases the loss, which is the shortest path across the decision boundary. With ε tiny (imperceptible), 'panda 57.7%' becomes 'gibbon 99.3%'. In the real world, stickers on stop signs or printed patterns can work too.",
   ["Correct: an input-gradient perturbation at test time.",
    "That's a backdoor attack (training-time poisoning).",
    "Retraining doesn't attack a model.",
    "Random removal isn't optimised."]],
  ["What is a backdoor attack",
   "An attacker injects a few poisoned training samples: clean images plus a small trigger (e.g. a yellow square) relabelled to a target class. The network learns 'trigger means target' as a shortcut while still learning the real task. On clean test data it looks perfect, so validation doesn't reveal the problem; at deployment anyone who adds the trigger controls the prediction. It's relevant when using public or community datasets, downloaded pre-trained weights, or outsourced training.",
   ["Correct: training data poisoned with a trigger.",
    "That's an adversarial attack.",
    "That's model theft.",
    "That's ordinary overfitting."]],
  ["What is an ensemble",
   "Train M models that differ (different random initialisations, data shuffles, bootstrap samples or architectures) and average their predicted probabilities. Errors that aren't shared partly cancel, so accuracy improves; the averaged probabilities are less extreme, so calibration improves; and disagreement between members signals uncertainty, which helps detect novel classes. The cost is M times the compute. Random forests are ensembles of trees.",
   ["Correct: averaging diverse models.",
    "Training longer isn't an ensemble.",
    "Depth isn't an ensemble.",
    "Multiple test sets isn't an ensemble."]],
  ["Which statement about explainable AI",
   "Explainability means a human can understand why a prediction was made, which matters for contestability (a refused mortgage), safety-critical decisions (medicine) and debugging (turtle classified as cat?). Decision trees are explainable by design (read the rules), though very deep trees become hard to read. CNNs with millions of weights are black boxes, so post-hoc tools are used: gradient saliency maps, occlusion maps, Grad-CAM, and feature visualisation. The lecture also asks whether we always need explanations, since we use ibuprofen without fully knowing how it works.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Deep learning is typically a black box.",
    "Trees are the classic explainable model.",
    "It matters most there."]],
  ["How is a gradient-based saliency",
   "Compute ∂s_c/∂x, the derivative of the class score s_c with respect to every input pixel, using backpropagation down to the image instead of stopping at the weights. Large absolute gradients mark pixels where a small change most affects the prediction. Display |gradient| (max over channels) as a heat map. Alternatives: occlusion maps (slide a grey patch and record the score drop) and Grad-CAM (gradient-weighted activation maps of the last conv layer).",
   ["Correct: the gradient of the class score with respect to the input.",
    "Not meaningful.",
    "That's an average image.",
    "Not an explanation method."]],
  ["Why can model.train() vs",
   "model.train() and model.eval() switch layer behaviour. Dropout(0.1) in train mode randomly zeroes 10% of activations; in eval mode it's disabled and all units are used. BatchNorm in train mode normalises with the current batch's mean and variance (and updates running averages); in eval mode it uses the stored running averages. Testing in train mode gives random, batch-dependent outputs. With a single test sample, batch statistics are meaningless and predictions can collapse. Always call model.eval() (and torch.no_grad()) for validation.",
   ["Correct.",
    "eval() changes behaviour, not weights.",
    "No layers are removed.",
    "The difference can be drastic."]],
  ["In the case study, a CNN could predict",
   "Researchers found CNNs could predict self-reported race from chest X-rays with high accuracy, though radiologists can't, and average images and saliency maps didn't reveal why. The concern: a diagnostic model might also use this hidden signal as a shortcut, so its errors could be distributed unequally across groups (bias) in a way nobody can see or explain. It illustrates why responsible AI principles (fairness, transparency, accountability) must apply along the whole value chain.",
   ["Correct: hidden demographic shortcuts create invisible bias risk.",
    "X-rays are widely used in ML.",
    "Accuracy isn't the concern here.",
    "CNNs handle grey-scale images fine."]],
  ["A classifier on 100 healthy patients",
   "Arrange the counts. Sick (positives): TP = 15, FN = 5, so 20 total. Healthy (negatives): TN = 90, FP = 10, so 100 total. Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) = 15/20 = 0.75. Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) = 90/100 = 0.90. Precision would be 15/(15+10) = 0.60 (one of the distractors). Always check which denominator belongs to which metric.",
   ["Correct: 0.75 and 0.90.",
    "Swapped.",
    "0.60 is precision, not sensitivity.",
    "Wrong denominators."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 6 ----------
  ["Which is a limitation of supervised learning that",
   "The supervised learning limitations slides: (1) dependence on the quantity and diversity of labelled data (11M-parameter models need many examples per class). (2) The model learns only features that discriminate the given labels, which may be shortcuts. (3) Label quality: noisy or inconsistent annotations. (4) Labels are expensive (expert time in medicine) and some data is too ambiguous or complex to label. Unsupervised and self-supervised learning learn structure from the far more abundant unlabelled data.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Supervised learning commonly uses neural nets.",
    "Classification is its main use.",
    "It requires labelled data."]],
  ["Which statement correctly distinguishes unsupervised",
   "Unsupervised: no labels at all; discover clusters (k-means, GMM), densities or low-dimensional structure (PCA, t-SNE). Self-supervised: still no human labels, but a supervised-style task is built from the data itself, e.g. predict the masked word, the next token, a missing patch, or whether two augmented views come from the same image. Semi-supervised: a small labelled set plus a large unlabelled set, e.g. pseudo-labelling and consistency regularisation.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Only supervised learning needs full labels.",
    "Self-supervised creates its own targets.",
    "It uses both labelled and unlabelled data."]],
  ["Which of the following statements about semi-supervised",
   "Semi-supervised learning assumes unlabelled data does carry information about the structure of p(x): the cluster assumption (points in the same cluster share a label) and the smoothness or manifold assumption. Methods propagate the few labels through that structure. It's suitable when labelling is expensive but raw data is plentiful (medical scans, web images), and it works for classification and regression.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's fully supervised learning.",
    "Unlabelled data is useful, which is the whole point.",
    "It also works for regression and other tasks."]],
  ["What are the steps of k-means",
   "Algorithm (Lloyd's): (1) initialise K centroids, e.g. K random data points. (2) Assignment: put each point in the cluster of its nearest centroid (Euclidean distance). (3) Update: move each centroid to the mean of its assigned points. (4) Repeat 2-3 until assignments stop changing. Each step decreases the within-cluster sum of squares Σ‖x − μ_k‖², so it always converges, but only to a local minimum. Run several random restarts or use k-means++ initialisation.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's kNN (supervised).",
    "That's a supervised Gaussian classifier.",
    "That's a decision tree."]],
  ["Which is a limitation of k-means",
   "Limitations: (1) K must be chosen in advance (elbow method, silhouette score). (2) Using the distance to a single centroid implies roughly spherical, similar-size clusters, so elongated or nested shapes fail. (3) The result depends on initialisation and can be a poor local minimum. (4) It's sensitive to outliers and feature scaling. Advantages: simple, fast, easy to implement and interpret. GMMs relax the spherical assumption with covariances.",
   ["Correct.",
    "It's simple and efficient (those are its advantages).",
    "It's unsupervised.",
    "It only finds local optima."]],
  ["What is a Gaussian Mixture Model",
   "A GMM models p(x) = Σ_k π_k N(x | μ_k, Σ_k), with mixing weights π_k summing to 1. Each Gaussian can have its own shape (covariance) and size. Each point gets a responsibility γ_ik = P(cluster k | x_i), a soft membership, rather than a hard label. Historically Pearson (1894) fitted two Gaussians to Weldon's crab measurements to test for two sub-species. GMMs are also used for brain tissue segmentation (grey matter, white matter, CSF intensities).",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not a neural network.",
    "Not a tree.",
    "That's k-means (hard assignment)."]],
  ["How does Expectation-Maximisation",
   "Initialise μ_k, σ_k, π_k. E-step: for each sample compute responsibilities γ_ik = π_k N(x_i|μ_k, σ_k) / Σ_j π_j N(x_i|μ_j, σ_j). M-step: re-estimate with weighted MLE: N_k = Σ_i γ_ik; μ_k = Σ_i γ_ik x_i / N_k; σ²_k = Σ_i γ_ik (x_i − μ_k)² / N_k; π_k = N_k / N. Repeat until the log-likelihood stops increasing (it never decreases). k-means is the limit of EM with hard 0/1 responsibilities and equal spherical variances.",
   ["Correct.",
    "EM isn't gradient descent with Adam.",
    "There's no closed form in one step; EM iterates.",
    "Random labels aren't EM."]],
  ["For n independent samples",
   "Derivation: the likelihood is L(μ, σ) = Πᵢ (1/√(2πσ²)) exp(−(xᵢ−μ)²/(2σ²)). The negative log-likelihood is NLL = (n/2)log(2πσ²) + Σᵢ(xᵢ−μ)²/(2σ²). (1) ∂NLL/∂μ = −Σᵢ(xᵢ−μ)/σ² = 0 gives μ̂ = (1/n)Σᵢxᵢ, the sample mean. (2) ∂NLL/∂σ = n/σ − Σᵢ(xᵢ−μ̂)²/σ³ = 0 gives σ̂² = (1/n)Σᵢ(xᵢ−μ̂)². Since μ̂ was estimated from the same data, σ̂² is biased low; the unbiased version divides by n − 1. If σ is fixed at 1, minimising the NLL over μ is exactly least squares, which links MLE to MSE.",
   ["Correct: the sample mean and the 1/n variance.",
    "The median and range aren't MLEs for a Gaussian.",
    "Those are a standard normal, not estimates.",
    "Missing the 1/n normalisation."]],
  ["What does maximum likelihood estimation seek",
   "Given data x₁…xₙ and a model family p(x|θ), the likelihood L(θ) = Πᵢ p(xᵢ|θ) says how probable the observed data is under θ. MLE picks θ̂ = argmax L(θ). Since products of small numbers underflow and are awkward to differentiate, we minimise the negative log-likelihood −Σᵢ log p(xᵢ|θ). Cross-entropy and MSE losses are both NLLs under categorical and Gaussian models, and the VAE and diffusion models are motivated by maximum likelihood.",
   ["Correct.",
    "We maximise likelihood, not loss.",
    "That's regularisation or Occam's razor.",
    "Labels are the data, not what's estimated."]],
  ["Which is an application of unsupervised clustering",
   "Anomaly detection: points far from every cluster (fraud, faults). Recommender systems: group users or items with similar behaviour. Exploring data that can't be labelled or is too big to label: customer segmentation, document topics. Medical imaging: GMM-based tissue segmentation of MRI into grey matter, white matter and CSF. Clustering also gives initial pseudo-labels for semi-supervised learning.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's optimisation.",
    "That's a transformer component.",
    "That's hyperparameter tuning."]],
  ["What is pseudo-labelling",
   "Procedure: (1) train a model on the small labelled set. (2) Predict the unlabelled data. (3) Keep predictions above a confidence threshold as pseudo-labels. (4) Retrain on labelled plus pseudo-labelled data, and iterate. The limitation is confirmation bias: confident but wrong pseudo-labels are learned as truth and reinforced, especially when the initial model is weak or classes are imbalanced. Calibration matters, because deep nets are over-confident.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not about humans relabelling.",
    "Not random shuffling.",
    "It uses unlabelled data."]],
  ["What is consistency regularisation",
   "Idea: the label of an image shouldn't change if it's flipped, cropped or colour-jittered, so the model's prediction shouldn't either. Add an unsupervised loss on unlabelled data that penalises the difference between predictions on two perturbed versions (e.g. MSE or KL between outputs; FixMatch uses a weak-augmentation pseudo-label for the strong-augmentation prediction). Key considerations: the augmentations must preserve the class, the strength of the perturbation, and the weight of the consistency loss.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not weight tying.",
    "Unrelated to the learning rate.",
    "It uses unlabelled data."]],
  ["In contrastive learning with data augmentation",
   "Pipeline (SimCLR-style): (1) take a reference image, create two random augmentations: a positive pair. (2) Other images in the batch are negatives. (3) Pass everything through an encoder (CNN) plus a projection to get embeddings. (4) A contrastive loss (InfoNCE, a margin loss) increases the similarity of positives and decreases it for negatives. After training, the encoder is a rich feature extractor; add a small head and fine-tune with few labels for downstream tasks.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Uses no human labels.",
    "Not dataset splits.",
    "Pairs come from real images."]],
  ["Which is a limitation of contrastive learning",
   "Challenges listed: (1) what augmentations to apply and how strong; too weak teaches nothing and too strong destroys semantics (a crop missing the object). (2) It needs many, and hard, negatives, so large batches or memory banks and long training. (3) 'Negatives' may actually be the same class (false negatives). These motivate non-contrastive methods (BYOL, DINO, JEPA) that avoid explicit negatives.",
   ["Correct.",
    "It's self-supervised.",
    "It uses neural encoders.",
    "It has many hyperparameters."]],
  ["Which is an example of predictive self-supervised",
   "Predictive self-supervision hides part of the data and predicts it. Text: next-token prediction (GPT) and masked-token prediction (BERT). Images: masked autoencoders (MAE) reconstruct masked patches; other tasks predict rotation angle, colourisation or patch order. Video and audio: predict future frames. The model must understand context to predict well, so it learns useful representations. The limitation: pixel-level prediction can waste capacity on irrelevant detail, which is the motivation for JEPA.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's supervised learning.",
    "That's supervised kNN.",
    "That's hyperparameter tuning."]],
  ["What makes a model a 'foundation model'",
   "Foundation models are large models pre-trained (usually self-supervised) on massive, broad data, then adapted to many downstream tasks by fine-tuning, linear probing, or prompting. Examples: DINOv2 (vision features usable for classification, segmentation and depth) and LLMs. Advantages: strong general features and very little task-specific labelled data needed. Dangers: they inherit biases from web-scale data, are opaque, are expensive to train (few organisations can), and errors propagate to every downstream use.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's a layer, not a model category.",
    "That's a narrow task-specific model.",
    "Adaptability is the defining feature."]],
  ["A model trained on Dataset A",
   "Step 1: replace the final layer (1000 outputs) with a new one (10 outputs). Its weights are random. Step 2, freezing: set requires_grad = False on the backbone and train only the head. This is fast, needs little data, and keeps the old knowledge. Fine-tuning: train all (or the upper) layers, typically with a smaller learning rate so pre-trained features aren't destroyed. It's better when Dataset B is large or different from A. It's a balance between new and old knowledge.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Reversed.",
    "Both keep the pre-trained weights.",
    "Input channels are unchanged."]],
  ["Which statement about t-SNE",
   "t-SNE converts high-dimensional distances into neighbour probabilities p_ij (Gaussian kernel), then places points in 2D with probabilities q_ij (heavy-tailed Student-t kernel) and moves them to minimise KL(P‖Q). It preserves local neighbourhoods and reveals clusters, but distances between clusters and cluster sizes aren't meaningful. It's non-parametric (no reusable mapping) and slow. UMAP (Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection) is similar but faster and preserves more global structure. PCA is linear.",
   ["Correct.",
    "PCA is linear; t-SNE is non-linear.",
    "It's an unsupervised visualisation method.",
    "Unrelated to GANs."]],
  ["Which property of the Kullback",
   "D_KL(p‖q) = ∫p(x) log(p(x)/q(x)) dx. The lecture builds it up. Naïve 1, the difference p − q, can cancel. Naïve 2, the ratio p/q, should be 1. Naïve 3, log(p/q), should be 0, weighted by p to focus where the data is. By Gibbs' inequality, KL ≥ 0, with equality only if p = q. It's not symmetric: KL(p‖q) punishes q being small where p is large, while KL(q‖p) does the opposite. So it isn't a distance (no symmetry, no triangle inequality).",
   ["Correct: not symmetric.",
    "KL ≥ 0.",
    "Neither symmetric nor a metric.",
    "It compares distributions, not samples."]],
  ["Which statement about k-means is NOT true",
   "Check each statement. K is set by the user (true); results depend on the initial centroids (true); each point goes to its nearest centroid (true). 'Requires labelled data' is false: k-means only uses the feature vectors and finds groups by distance. The names are confusingly similar: kNN (supervised) needs labels to vote; k-means (unsupervised) doesn't.",
   ["True: K is a hyperparameter.",
    "True: there are local minima.",
    "NOT true, so this is the answer: k-means is unsupervised.",
    "True: that's the assignment step."]],
  ["Consider the statements:\nA/ GMM-EM",
   "A: the E-step computes probabilistic responsibilities, so assignments are soft. True. B: k-means assigns each point to exactly one cluster (hard), so false. C: EM alternates the E-step (responsibilities) and M-step (parameter updates), so true. D: EM fits GMMs without labels (unsupervised), so false. Answer: A and C.",
   ["Correct: A and C.",
    "B is false.",
    "D is false.",
    "B and D are both false."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 7 ----------
  ["What is time-series data",
   "A time series is x₁, x₂, …, x_T ordered in time, where each value is correlated with previous ones (autocorrelation, trends, seasonality). Shuffling the order destroys information. Unlike a single image classified independently, the model must remember context over time. Text can be treated as a time series of tokens (T, T+1, T+2…), which is why the same sequence models (RNN, LSTM, transformer) apply to language, audio, sensors and finance.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's i.i.d. data without order.",
    "Not a definition of time series.",
    "Temporal structure is the defining feature."]],
  ["Which is an application of time-series",
   "Forecasting predicts future values: stock prices, energy demand, weather, sales. Prediction or classification labels a sequence or each step: activity recognition from accelerometers, ECG arrhythmia detection, speech recognition, machine translation. Segmenting a single photo, clustering flowers and confusion matrices don't involve temporal order.",
   ["Correct.",
    "A single image isn't a time series.",
    "Static tabular data.",
    "An evaluation tool."]],
  ["What is the trade-off between character-level",
   "Character tokens: vocabulary ≈ 65 (Shakespeare) gives tiny embedding and output layers, no unknown words, but 1,115,000 tokens for the text, so long sequences and costly attention (quadratic in length). Word tokens: sequences are ~5× shorter, but the vocabulary is tens of thousands and rare words are unknown. Subword tokenisers (BPE, SentencePiece) are the compromise: frequent words are single tokens and rare words split into pieces. GPT-style vocabularies are ~50,000 tokens.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Characters give a small vocabulary.",
    "There is a clear trade-off.",
    "A large vocabulary means large embedding and output matrices."]],
  ["In the Shakespeare example",
   "Count it: 26 lowercase + 26 uppercase = 52 letters, plus space, newline (\\n), and punctuation such as ! $ & ' , - . 3 : ; ?, giving 65 unique characters. Each gets an integer id (0-64) for the lookup table. A model then predicts a distribution over 65 symbols at each step.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's characters, not words.",
    "The embedding dimension is a design choice.",
    "Heads are an architecture choice."]],
  ["How does a bigram language model",
   "A bigram model stores a V×V matrix of P(next | current), counted from text or learned as weights (in the tutorial, a learned matrix with random initial weights). Generation: start with a token, sample the next from its row, repeat. It only sees one previous token, so output is 'not random but still gibberish'. N-grams with N > 2 condition on more context, but the table grows as V^N, which quickly becomes intractable. That motivates embeddings and neural models.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's a transformer.",
    "That's retrieval.",
    "Text isn't processed as an image here."]],
  ["What is a disadvantage of the Bag of Words",
   "BoW represents a sentence as a vector of word counts over the vocabulary: 'I am very very tired today' sets very = 2. It's easy to compute and handles variable-length text, but (1) it loses order: 'not happy because raining' vs 'happy because not raining' can look the same; (2) the vector is as long as the vocabulary (very high-dimensional and sparse); (3) it has no notion of word similarity. Lookup-table embeddings fix (2) and (3), and positional encoding or recurrence fixes (1).",
   ["Correct.",
    "It's very easy to calculate.",
    "No GPU is needed.",
    "Counting repeats is exactly what it does."]],
  ["What is a token embedding",
   "An embedding table is a learnable matrix E of size V × d. Token id i selects row Eᵢ, a dense d-dimensional vector. During training, tokens used in similar contexts get similar vectors, so geometry encodes meaning: clusters of related words, and directions like king − man + woman ≈ queen. Word2Vec learns them by predicting context words; in LLMs they're learned jointly with the transformer. It's far more compact and meaningful than one-hot or BoW vectors.",
   ["Correct.",
    "One-hot vectors don't encode similarity.",
    "That's positional encoding.",
    "That's the model output."]],
  ["How is a recurrent neural network (RNN) different",
   "At each step t the RNN computes h_t = f(W_x x_t + W_h h_(t−1) + b) and the output y_t = g(W_y h_t). The hidden state h carries a summary of everything seen so far, and the same weights are reused at every step, so any sequence length works. Configurations: many-to-many (translation, tagging), many-to-one (sentiment), one-to-many (captioning, music generation). Training uses backpropagation through time.",
   ["Correct.",
    "It has weights, shared over time.",
    "That's a transformer.",
    "It's mainly for sequences."]],
  ["Which is NOT a limitation of RNNs",
   "RNN limitations: (1) sequential computation: h_t needs h_(t−1), so you can't parallelise across time on GPUs, which makes training slow. (2) Vanishing or exploding gradients: backprop through time multiplies by W_h repeatedly. (3) Long-range context is lost; e.g. 'On the weekend, I went to the shops to buy an ice-cream … it was delicious', where the link spans many steps. Processing sequences is the RNN's purpose, not a limitation.",
   ["NOT a limitation, so this is the answer: RNNs process sequences.",
    "A real limitation.",
    "A real limitation.",
    "A real limitation."]],
  ["What problem do LSTM networks",
   "In a vanilla RNN the gradient through time is a product of many Jacobians, so it vanishes (or explodes) over long sequences. The LSTM adds a cell state c_t updated additively: c_t = f_t ⊙ c_(t−1) + i_t ⊙ c̃_t. When the forget gate f_t ≈ 1, information and gradient flow through many steps almost unchanged (like a residual connection through time). LSTMs aren't faster or lighter; they're slower and use more memory, and they rely on sigmoid and tanh activations.",
   ["LSTMs are slower than plain RNNs.",
    "They use more memory (4 gate matrices).",
    "They depend on sigmoid and tanh activations.",
    "Correct: they solve vanishing gradients over long sequences."]],
  ["What regulates the cell state",
   "Gates are sigmoid layers outputting values in [0, 1] that multiply information element-wise. Forget gate f_t: how much of the old cell state to keep. Input gate i_t: how much of the new candidate c̃_t (tanh) to write. Output gate o_t: how much of tanh(c_t) to expose as the hidden state h_t. This learned control gives both long-term memory (cell state) and short-term memory (hidden state), hence 'Long Short-Term Memory'.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Softmax is used at the output layer.",
    "That's for transformers.",
    "Pooling is a CNN component."]],
  ["What is a remaining limitation of LSTMs",
   "LSTMs fix much of the vanishing gradient problem, but they're still recurrent: step t must wait for t − 1, so they can't use GPU parallelism across the sequence and are slow to train on large corpora. Very long-range dependencies remain hard, and each step does 4 times the computation of an RNN. Transformers replace recurrence with attention, processing all positions in parallel.",
   ["Correct.",
    "They have a memory cell.",
    "They're trained with BPTT.",
    "They work on sequences."]],
  ["What is the key difference between the Transformer",
   "RNNs and LSTMs pass information step by step through a hidden state. A transformer feeds all tokens at once and uses self-attention: every token directly computes a weighted combination of all other tokens. So (1) training parallelises across the sequence (fast on GPUs), and (2) any two positions are connected in one step, capturing global context without information degrading over distance. Order information is injected by positional encoding.",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's an RNN.",
    "Transformers use attention plus MLP layers.",
    "They're the basis of LLMs."]],
  ["Which is a limitation of Transformers",
   "(1) Data hungry: with few built-in assumptions (no recurrence, no locality), they need huge datasets to generalise. (2) Self-attention computes a T × T matrix, so memory and compute grow as O(T²) with context length; 128k-token contexts are expensive. (3) Huge compute and energy to train. Mitigations: the KV cache for inference, sliding-window or linear attention, and efficient kernels.",
   ["Correct.",
    "They're highly parallel.",
    "They have billions of parameters.",
    "Capturing long-range context is their strength."]],
  ["Why is a positional encoding needed",
   "Self-attention is permutation-equivariant: it treats the input as a set. Shuffle the tokens and each output is the same, just shuffled. 'I'm not happy because it is raining' and 'I'm happy because it is not raining' contain the same tokens, so without position information they'd be indistinguishable. Adding a position vector PE(pos) to each token embedding makes identical tokens at different positions differ. The original transformer uses sinusoids: PE(pos, 2i) = sin(pos/10000^(2i/d)), PE(pos, 2i+1) = cos(pos/10000^(2i/d)).",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's layer norm.",
    "That's tokenisation.",
    "That's the causal mask."]],
  ["What does the attention mechanism compute",
   "Each token embedding x is projected into a query q = W_q x, key k = W_k x and value v = W_v x. For T tokens stacked: Q, K, V are T × D. Scores QKᵀ (T × T) measure how relevant token j is to token i. Divide by √D (keeps the softmax from saturating), apply softmax row-wise (each row sums to 1), multiply by V: output = softmax(QKᵀ/√D)V. Each token's new representation is a context-weighted mix of values, added back to its embedding, so tokens get 'nudged' towards their meaning in context.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Attention weights depend on queries and keys.",
    "Not a convolution.",
    "Class probabilities come from the final layer."]],
  ["Consider 4 concepts: 1. Multi-Head",
   "Match one at a time. Multi-head attention runs several smaller attentions in parallel on split dimensions, so 1-D. Value V holds the content that the attention weights combine: the scores determine how much each value contributes, so 2-C. Self-attention means each token attends to all tokens of the same sequence, so 3-A. Causal masking sets future positions to −∞ before softmax so token t only sees ≤ t, needed for autoregressive generation, so 4-B. Answer: 1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B.",
   ["Correct: 1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B.",
    "Takes the statements in order, which is wrong.",
    "Swaps the Value and Causal Masking statements.",
    "Mismatches several."]],
  ["With dim_emb = 1024",
   "One head with D = 1024 creates large Q, K, V matrices and a single attention pattern. Multi-head splits D into h heads of size D/h (e.g. 16 heads × 64). Each head has its own projections and attention, so one head can track syntax and another coreference. Total compute is similar to one full-size head. The h outputs (T × 64 each) are concatenated back to T × 1024 and passed through a final linear projection W_o.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Vocabulary is unrelated.",
    "Positions are still needed.",
    "Still not recurrent."]],
  ["How are decoder-only transformers",
   "Take a huge text corpus, tokenise it, and slice windows of length T. The input is tokens 1…T and the target is tokens 2…T+1 (shifted by one). A causal mask stops position t seeing the future, so the model learns T next-token predictions per window with cross-entropy loss. No human labels are needed; the text supervises itself. BERT-style encoders instead mask random tokens and predict them using both sides of the context. Instruction tuning and RLHF come after pre-training.",
   ["Correct: self-supervised next-token prediction.",
    "Pre-training uses no human labels.",
    "Not clustering.",
    "LLMs are pre-trained on text."]],
  ["What does the final layer of a language",
   "After N blocks, each position has a d-dimensional vector. A linear layer (d → V, e.g. V ≈ 50,000) produces one logit per vocabulary token, and softmax turns them into next-token probabilities. Generation picks (greedy) or samples (temperature, top-k) a token, appends it to the input, and repeats (autoregressive). It's literally a linear classifier over the vocabulary.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not pooling.",
    "Not clustering.",
    "PE is added at the input."]],
  ["What is the purpose of the KV cache",
   "During generation, step t needs attention between the new token's query and the keys and values of all previous tokens. Those K and V vectors don't change once computed, so storing them avoids recomputing T projections per layer at each step. The cost per new token goes from O(T²) to O(T). The price is memory: the cache grows with context length × layers × heads, which is why long-context inference is memory-bound.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not the dataset.",
    "Gradients aren't computed at inference.",
    "The vocabulary is the embedding table."]],
  ["According to the lecture, how many transformer layers",
   "These are facts from the slides: GPT-3 stacks 96 transformer blocks; DeepSeek-R1 has 61. Each block is multi-head attention + residual + layer norm + feed-forward MLP + residual + layer norm. Depth lets the model refine token representations many times. Exams may ask for such numbers, so note them on your one-page summary.",
   ["Correct: 96 and 61.",
    "Far too small.",
    "Both are deep.",
    "Swapped."]],
  ["Consider the 4 statements:\nA/ Transformers",
   "A: transformers feed all tokens simultaneously, true. B: RNNs are sequential (h_t depends on h_(t−1)), so they can't be parallelised over time, false. C: attention is permutation-equivariant, so positional encoding is needed, true. D: LSTMs rely on sigmoid gates and tanh activations, false. Answer: A and C.",
   ["Correct.",
    "B is false.",
    "B and D are false.",
    "D is false."]],
  ["A transformer processes a sequence of T = 9",
   "Shapes: X is T × d = 9 × 16. Q = XW_q is 9 × D, K is 9 × D. QKᵀ = (9 × D)(D × 9) = 9 × 9. One score for each (query token, key token) pair. Softmax keeps the shape; A·V = (9 × 9)(9 × D) gives 9 × D outputs. The 9 × 9 size (T²) is why attention is quadratic in sequence length.",
   ["Correct: T × T.",
    "That's D × D, the weight matrix size.",
    "That's the shape of Q or the output.",
    "Transposed shape of Q."]],
  ["An embedding dimension of 768",
   "Per-head dimension = d_model / h = 768 / 12 = 64. Each head projects to 64-dim Q, K, V; attention runs per head; the 12 outputs of size 64 are concatenated: 12 × 64 = 768. This is GPT-2 small / BERT-base sizing.",
   ["Correct: 768/12 = 64.",
    "12 is the number of heads.",
    "That's the full model dimension.",
    "Multiplied instead of divided."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 8 ----------
  ["What is the difference between discriminative",
   "A discriminative model learns p(y|x) directly: the boundary between classes (logistic regression, CNN classifiers). A generative model learns how the data itself is distributed, p(x) or the joint p(x, y). Using p(x, y) = p(y|x)p(x), knowing p(x) adds information: a point can have p(red|x) high (far on the red side of the boundary) while p(x) is tiny (unlike any data), so the decision is actually uncertain. Generative models can also sample new data from p(x).",
   ["Correct.",
    "Reversed.",
    "Discriminative models learn p(y|x).",
    "Modern generative models are neural."]],
  ["What is the manifold hypothesis",
   "Count the space: a 3×32×32 image with 256 levels per pixel has 256³⁰⁷² possible images, almost all of which look like noise. Real images occupy a tiny, structured, low-dimensional subset: a manifold with a few intrinsic degrees of freedom (pose, lighting, shape). The hypothesis justifies dimensionality reduction. PCA assumes the manifold is linear (a flat subspace); autoencoders and VAEs learn curved, non-linear manifolds.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Real data is concentrated, not uniform.",
    "Unrelated.",
    "Real images are highly structured."]],
  ["Considering the principal component analysis",
   "PCA: centre the data, compute the covariance C = (1/N)XᵀX, find its eigenvectors uᵢ (directions) and eigenvalues λᵢ (variance along each). The eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix are orthogonal (A true), λᵢ is the variance captured and Σλᵢ = trace(C) = total variance (B true), and the directions are eigenvectors of C (C true). D is false: SVD of X is one convenient, numerically stable way (the right singular vectors are the uᵢ, and λᵢ = σᵢ²/N), but eigendecomposition of C or iterative methods work too.",
   ["True: the components are orthogonal.",
    "True: the variances sum to the total variance.",
    "True: they're eigenvectors of the covariance.",
    "NOT true, so this is the answer: SVD is optional."]],
  ["How are the principal components ordered",
   "PCA maximises variance step by step: u₁ = argmax over ‖u‖=1 of uᵀCu gives the top eigenvector, with variance λ₁ (the largest eigenvalue); u₂ is the best direction orthogonal to u₁, and so on. Keeping the first d components keeps the most variance possible for any d-dim linear projection; the fraction explained is (λ₁+…+λ_d)/Σλᵢ. That's how you pick d (e.g. 95% of variance).",
   ["Correct.",
    "There's a definite order.",
    "Reversed.",
    "Not meaningful."]],
  ["How does PCA relate to an autoencoder",
   "Keep the top d eigenvectors as the columns of U (D × d). Encoder: z = Uᵀx (d numbers). Decoder: x̂ = Uz = UUᵀx (the projection back into the subspace). That's a linear autoencoder with tied weights, and a linear AE trained with MSE learns the same subspace. An autoencoder replaces Uᵀ and U with non-linear networks Enc and Dec, so it can follow curved manifolds; locally, its tangent space behaves like PCA.",
   ["Correct.",
    "PCA isn't adversarial.",
    "They're closely related.",
    "Reversed: the AE is the non-linear generalisation."]],
  ["If the data space has 10",
   "Centred data X is N × D (50,000 × 10⁶). Its covariance has rank ≤ min(N − 1, D) ≈ 50,000. All the data lies in the span of the 50,000 samples, so at most ~50,000 non-zero eigenvalues and meaningful components exist; the remaining directions have zero variance in the sample. In practice you keep far fewer (e.g. a few hundred).",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not with only 50,000 samples.",
    "No reason for 2.",
    "Impossible."]],
  ["How is an autoencoder trained",
   "Architecture: x goes through Enc to a low-dimensional bottleneck z, then through Dec to x̂. Loss: reconstruction error, e.g. MSE ‖x − x̂‖² (or BCE for binary pixels), minimised by backprop over both networks. The bottleneck forces the network to keep only the most important information: compression, so information is lost. Since the target is the input, any unlabelled dataset works, making it self-supervised. The latent z is a learned non-linear coordinate on the data manifold.",
   ["Correct.",
    "No labels are used.",
    "Not a classifier.",
    "KL is added in VAEs, not plain AEs."]],
  ["What is a denoising autoencoder",
   "Training pairs: input x̃ = x + noise (e.g. N(0,1) or masking), target x (clean). The network can't copy its input, so it must learn what clean data looks like and project corrupted points back onto the manifold. It learns robust features and is a practical denoiser. Conceptually it's the ancestor of diffusion models, which learn to denoise at many noise levels.",
   ["Correct.",
    "It has a decoder.",
    "Reversed.",
    "Not a discriminator."]],
  ["What is a sparse autoencoder",
   "Instead of compressing through a narrow bottleneck, the latent can be large (e.g. 1×784 for a 1×28×28 MNIST image, the same size as the input). To prevent trivial copying, a sparsity constraint is added: an L1 penalty λ‖z‖₁ on activations, or top-k (keep the k largest activations, zero the rest). Each input is then explained by a few active features, which are easier to interpret. That's why sparse AEs are used to study the features inside LLMs.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not about dataset size.",
    "The input is dense.",
    "That wouldn't learn anything."]],
  ["What is a masked autoencoder",
   "Randomly hide a large fraction of input patches (MAE masks ~75%), encode only the visible ones, and train the decoder to reconstruct the missing pixels. To fill in the gaps, the model must understand object structure and context, so the encoder learns strong representations for downstream tasks. It's a predictive self-supervised task, analogous to BERT's masked language modelling.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Masks are on the data, not weights.",
    "That's for autoregressive transformers.",
    "No labels are used."]],
  ["Why is a standard autoencoder a poor",
   "The AE only learns to map training images to some codes and back. Nothing constrains how codes are spread: they may sit in scattered islands with gaps, at arbitrary scales. If you sample a random z, it's likely to land in a gap the decoder never saw, giving rubbish output. The VAE fixes this by forcing the latent distribution towards a known prior N(0, I), so sampling from the prior gives decodable codes.",
   ["Correct.",
    "The decoder outputs images.",
    "AEs train fine.",
    "The latent can be any size."]],
  ["What is the loss function of a VAE",
   "L = E_q[‖x − x̂‖²] + β·D_KL(q(z|x) ‖ N(0, I)). Reconstruction makes decoded samples match the input; KL pulls each encoded distribution N(μ, σ²) towards the standard normal so the latent space is filled smoothly and can be sampled. For Gaussian q and prior, the KL has a closed form per latent dimension: D_KL = ½Σ(μ² + σ² − log σ² − 1). β balances the two terms (β = 1 is the standard ELBO).",
   ["Correct.",
    "Missing the reconstruction term.",
    "That's the GAN loss.",
    "Hinge isn't used."]],
  ["What is the reparameterisation trick",
   "Sampling z ~ N(μ, σ²) is random, so you can't backprop through 'draw a sample'. Rewrite: ε ~ N(0, 1) (no parameters), z = μ + σ·ε. Now z is a deterministic, differentiable function of μ and σ, with ∂z/∂μ = 1 and ∂z/∂σ = ε. Gradients from the reconstruction loss reach the encoder. Implementation: std = exp(0.5·logvar); z = mu + std * torch.randn_like(std).",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's an autoencoder with no bottleneck.",
    "Not used.",
    "The prior is Gaussian."]],
  ["In the MNIST VAE implementation",
   "For a latent of dimension d, the encoder's last layer outputs 2d numbers: d for μ and d for log σ². Why log-variance? σ must be positive, and predicting log σ² lets the network output any real number; σ = exp(0.5·logvar) is always positive and numerically stable. The KL term also uses log σ² directly: −½Σ(1 + logvar − μ² − exp(logvar)).",
   ["Correct.",
    "That's the decoder's job.",
    "Not used in a plain VAE.",
    "It needs a vector."]],
  ["What does the Kullback",
   "D_KL(p‖q) = ∫p(x) log(p(x)/q(x)) dx = E_p[log p − log q]. It's the expected extra information (in nats or bits) needed when using q to describe data that actually comes from p. Properties: ≥ 0, = 0 iff p = q, not symmetric. In the VAE, p = q_φ(z|x) (the encoder) and q = N(0, I) (the prior). In t-SNE it compares high- and low-dimensional neighbour distributions.",
   ["Correct.",
    "Not a Euclidean distance.",
    "Not accuracy.",
    "Not a parameter count."]],
  ["Why does the VAE maximise the Evidence",
   "We want max log p(x), but p(x) = ∫p(x|z)p(z)dz integrates over the unknown latent manifold, which is intractable. Introduce the encoder q_φ(z|x). Then log p(x) = ELBO + D_KL(q_φ(z|x) ‖ p(z|x)). Since KL ≥ 0, log p(x) ≥ ELBO, where ELBO = E_q[log p_θ(x|z)] − D_KL(q_φ(z|x) ‖ p(z)). The first term is reconstruction (MSE for a Gaussian decoder), the second the prior-matching term. Maximising the ELBO trains the encoder and decoder together.",
   ["Correct.",
    "log p(x) is a real, informative quantity.",
    "Not accuracy.",
    "It is maximum likelihood, made tractable."]],
  ["What happens if the KL term weight",
   "β sets the trade-off. β too large (×10): the KL dominates, every q(z|x) collapses towards N(0, I), the latent histograms look perfectly Gaussian, but the codes carry little information about x, so reconstructions are blurry or average (posterior collapse is the extreme case: μ = 0, σ = 1 for all inputs). β too small (÷10): it behaves like a plain autoencoder, with good reconstructions but a latent that doesn't match the prior, so random samples are poor. The lecture also notes the other extreme, σ → 0 ('autoencoder mode').",
   ["Correct.",
    "That describes β too small.",
    "β strongly changes behaviour.",
    "No discriminator is involved."]],
  ["What is a conditional VAE",
   "Concatenate (or embed) a condition c, such as a one-hot class label, to the encoder input and the decoder input: q(z|x, c), p(x|z, c). The latent then only needs to encode the style (stroke thickness, slant), while c encodes the content (which digit). To generate a '7', sample z ~ N(0, I), set c = 7 and decode. It also fixes the multi-manifold ridge problem by giving each class its own Gaussian region. Classification trick: decode with each of the 10 conditions and pick the lowest reconstruction error.",
   ["Correct.",
    "'Conditional' refers to an input condition.",
    "It keeps both encoder and decoder.",
    "Causal masks are for transformers."]],
  ["Why can a trained VAE be used",
   "The KL term pushes the aggregate latent distribution towards N(0, I), so most of that region decodes into plausible images. Generation: z ~ N(0, I), x = Dec(z), with no encoder needed. You can also interpolate smoothly between codes. Limitations: samples are often blurry, the encoded μ manifold doesn't perfectly cover the prior, and multiple classes can create ridges in z.",
   ["Correct.",
    "It doesn't memorise; it decodes new z.",
    "That's a GAN.",
    "Neural encoders aren't invertible analytically."]],
  ["Consider the 4 statements about VAEs",
   "A: KL(p‖q) ≠ KL(q‖p) in general, so false. B: the encoder outputs μ and ln σ² (2 × latent dim), so true. C: reparameterisation z = μ + σ·ε, ε ~ N(0,1), so true. D: a plain AE puts no constraint on its latent distribution, so false (that's why it's a poor generator). Answer: B and C.",
   ["Correct: B and C.",
    "A is false.",
    "A and D are false.",
    "D is false."]]
);
